"Connects binned frequencies at numeric bin midpoints to compare distribution shapes with less filled overlap.\n\nUse when: Several distributions share common bin boundaries and need shape comparison. A line representation makes overlapping frequency profiles easier to read.\n\nAvoid when: Very few observations make a connected profile look falsely smooth.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal positions encode bin midpoints. Vertical positions encode counts or normalized frequency for each bin.\n\nBuild: Create identical bins for all groups and calculate their midpoints. Choose counts or proportions according to sample-size comparability. Connect midpoint frequencies and state whether zero endpoints close the profile.\n\nCheck: Verify equal bin widths when comparing frequency heights. Check normalization separately within each group if proportions are used. Identify the exact binning scheme in an annotation or subtitle.\n\nPitfalls: Segments interpolate between bin centers without measuring intermediate values. Crossing profiles reflect real frequency differences; use separate panels if individual lines become difficult to trace.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic parcel weights for two depots using identical bins and normalized midpoint lines.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, Density plot, Ridgeline plot."
